WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.380
Ah! The good old days before electricity.

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:10.560
Did we actually have it better before the first power plant started running?

00:00:11.495 --> 00:00:14.935
What if everyone on Earth tried getting by with all their lights turned off?

00:00:15.195 --> 00:00:18.065
We would start to challenge with a perfect Earth-hour,

00:00:18.065 --> 00:00:20.960
sixty minutes of complete darkness, no artificial light allowed.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.845
Would we reduce the light pollution enough to

00:00:23.845 --> 00:00:26.000
be able to see the Milky way in the night sky?

00:00:26.515 --> 00:00:28.455
How much electricity would we save?

00:00:28.965 --> 00:00:31.095
Would it help us fight climate change?

00:00:32.035 --> 00:00:35.515
This is What IF and here's what would happen if everyone on the planet

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.340
switched off their lights for an hour.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:43.860
Humanity has spent the last one hundred years increasing our dependence on electricity.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.380
Look around, you computer, microwave, fridge, elevator, traffic lights,

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.660
public transportation... Everything relies on electrical power.

00:00:51.575 --> 00:00:54.915
Going completely without power even for an hour would be pretty uncomfortable.

00:00:55.295 --> 00:00:58.065
So, let's just limit humanity's light usage.

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.080
Hit the lights... Let's begin.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.440
On average humanity consumes 62,000 terajoules of electricity

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:05.940
per hour!!

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.020
That's how energy your gas powered car would need to drive to Pluto

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:10.540
and back.

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:14.340
Twice!!! One-fifth of all this energy goes towards lighting.

00:01:14.665 --> 00:01:17.565
That's a lot of energy and producing it means spilling a

00:01:17.565 --> 00:01:19.635
lot of carbon-dioxide into the atmosphere.

00:01:19.925 --> 00:01:23.005
Obviously clean energy from the sun, wind and water

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.580
don't effect the CO2 concentration in the air

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.340
but they only make up one-third of

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.420
global electricity production. The rest of it releases

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:31.340
green-house gas emissions.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:34.620
The thinking here is, if we gave our power plants an hour break,

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.780
we would significantly reduce those CO2 emissions on a global level.

00:01:38.180 --> 00:01:40.260
What an easy way to fight climate change together?

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:42.260
or is it?

00:01:42.885 --> 00:01:45.885
Turns out it's rather complicated. If this world-wide

00:01:45.885 --> 00:01:48.825
earth hour cut to at night getting by with the light of some cozy candles

00:01:48.825 --> 00:01:51.120
is not the best idea.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:53.960
Not only are they a 100 times less efficient than your light bulb

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.720
but guess what, they also emit CO2 .

00:01:57.260 --> 00:02:00.285
In theory you can even the score by using just one candle

00:02:00.285 --> 00:02:03.360
for an light bulb you've turned off but if you lit up twice as many

00:02:03.365 --> 00:02:05.835
as candles you'd actually be emitting more CO2 .

00:02:06.215 --> 00:02:07.955
What if you didn't light any candles?

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:11.175
You might think we'd emit less carbon-dioxide into the air

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:14.120
but you would be wondering , Yeah that's not how power plants work?

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.360
The coal burning ones would in fact emit less CO2

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:20.540
during the dark hours but then, everyone would turn their lights back on.

00:02:20.775 --> 00:02:23.725
For power stations this sudden increasing in demand would

00:02:23.725 --> 00:02:25.355
mean firing up more generators.

00:02:25.715 --> 00:02:28.535
As a result, they would emit even more CO2

00:02:28.795 --> 00:02:32.035
and this extra carbon waste wold start to to trap more heat in our

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.960
lower atmosphere possibly speeding up climate change

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:38.360
and that's if power plants would be able to cope with such a rapid increase in demand.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:41.460
In a worse case scenario, they would need to perform an emergency

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.700
shut down, leaving us with no electricity

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:45.420
for days.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.985
When that happened you would at least get to enjoy a view of the Milky way at night.

00:02:48.985 --> 00:02:51.555
A view that light pollution prevents one-third of

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.240
the world's population from seeing.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.820
So may be you shouldn't plunge yourself back into darkness.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.920
There are better ways to fight climate change than turning off all the lights

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:02.840
at the same time, for starters you could try replacing all your

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:05.780
incandescent light bulbs with compact fluorescent ones

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.560
or take public transportation ward.

00:03:08.055 --> 00:03:10.375
What do you do to reduce your carbon-footprint?

